MOVES = [
("Move 1: underline the nouns. Every noun with its own state becomes a class.",
 "The game holds every token's position, the turn order and the status, all of which change every turn: a class, and "
 "the only thing here that owns anything that moves. The board holds its size and its jumps and never changes once it "
 "is built: a class. A snake and a ladder are the same object &mdash; a one-way teleport from one cell to another "
 "&mdash; and the only difference is whether the end is below the start, which is a sign, not a reason for two "
 "classes; so there is one <code>Jump</code> with a <code>kind()</code> that works it out, and a whole hierarchy dies "
 "before it is born. A player has an id and a name and nothing that moves, so it is a record, and the position lives "
 "on the game: that one decision is what makes the concurrency answer easy later. A turn is worth keeping, so it is a "
 "record too, and the dice and the rules own nothing at all, so they are interfaces.", 1),
("Move 2: for every verb, ask which class holds the state it touches. That class gets the method.",
 "The picture is the whole move; what matters is the last row. \"Move a token and pass the turn\" touches the "
 "positions, the queue, the status and the log at once. Only the game can see all four, so "
 "<code>game.roll(playerId)</code> is the orchestrator and everything it calls &mdash; the throw, the landing rule, "
 "the jump lookup &mdash; stays small and stateless. That is also the answer to the question a lot of candidates get "
 "wrong here: the position does not live on <code>Player</code>. Put it there and two phones can write the same field "
 "with no lock in sight; keep it on the game and the concurrency answer in move 4 is one sentence. And notice what is "
 "not a verb of its own: winning. Winning is a comparison the game makes on the cell the token came to rest on, which "
 "is why it is one line and not a class.", 2),
("Move 3: every rule the interviewer can change mid-round goes behind an interface and is handed in.",
 "Three things will change, and you can name them before they are asked. How you throw: one die today, three dice "
 "tomorrow, a scripted die in every test. How far a throw carries you: stay put on an overshoot today, bounce back "
 "tomorrow &mdash; and the house rule where a token sits off the board until you throw a six is the <i>same</i> "
 "interface, because it too only answers \"which cell does this throw put me on?\". Whether the maximum grants another "
 "turn: no today, yes with a cap tomorrow. Each is a one-method interface the game is <i>given</i> by its builder and "
 "never constructs. This is where the patterns come from, not the other way round: a swappable rule behind an "
 "interface is <b>Strategy</b>, and a rule that wraps another rule is <b>Decorator</b> &mdash; "
 "<code>CappedExtraTurn</code> adds \"not more than three in a row\" to any extra-turn rule, "
 "<code>MustRollSixToEnter</code> adds the entry gate to any move rule. A game that announces \"a turn happened\" "
 "without knowing what a screen is, is <b>Observer</b>. I do them; I do not announce them.", 3),
("Move 4: state that many callers change at the same time gets one owner and one lock.",
 "The single-process version of this game needs no lock at all, and saying so is worth a mark: one loop mutates "
 "everything, so adding <code>synchronized</code> would be cargo cult. The moment it is an app it changes. Bob has the "
 "game open on his phone and his tablet and presses roll on both. Both read \"it is Bob's turn\", both roll, both move "
 "his token: one turn, two moves &mdash; or worse, the queue rotates twice and somebody is skipped. So checking whose "
 "turn it is, throwing, moving and rotating must be <i>one</i> step, in the class that owns all of it: the game. The "
 "lock is per <i>game</i>, not per server, which is the whole trick &mdash; a million tables play in parallel and "
 "never wait for each other, while the four people at one table are serialised for half a microsecond. Anything that "
 "only listens (the commentary, the screens) is called after the lock is released, never inside it.", 4),
("Move 5: for each collection, ask what question is asked of it, and pick the shape that answers in O(1).",
 "\"What is on the cell I landed on?\" is a map from cell to jump. That is the shape worth defending: a hundred-cell "
 "board has about twenty interesting cells, so an array of a hundred <code>Cell</code> objects stores eighty empty "
 "ones to answer a question a map answers in one hop. (The honest counter-argument: if a UI arrives and every cell "
 "needs a picture and a colour, a dense array becomes the better home &mdash; say that and move on.) \"Whose turn is "
 "it?\" is a deque, not a list with an index somebody has to keep: <code>peek</code> to see, <code>poll</code> then "
 "<code>addLast</code> to rotate, both O(1), and the code reads like the domain. The one shape that is not O(1) is "
 "taking a player out of the middle when they quit, which is O(n) in the number of players &mdash; under ten, so it "
 "does not matter, and saying that is better than pretending it is a hash map.", 5),
("Move 6: anything with a life cycle is a state machine, and the order of operations is part of the design.",
 "The three states are in the picture; the six numbered steps beside them are the part to defend out loud, and two "
 "rules produce that order. <i>Check before you spend</i>: the state and the turn are checked before the die is "
 "touched, so a phone that rolls out of turn costs the game nothing &mdash; not a log row, not even a random number, "
 "which is what keeps a seeded game reproducible however many stray requests arrive. <i>Compute, then write</i>: the "
 "throw, the landing cell, the jump and the win all go into local variables and are checked against the board before "
 "the first <code>put</code>, so anything that throws half way leaves the game exactly as it was with the same player "
 "still due. The queue rotates last, or a reader could catch a token that has moved while its owner is still shown as "
 "due. Undo is that same list read upwards, which is only possible because every write went through one method in one "
 "order. And the board is validated once in <code>build()</code>, so the turn loop never checks a layout it was "
 "handed.", 6),
("Move 7: yes, the lock makes one table's turns happen one at a time. Ask for how long, and what is inside it.",
 "Inside the lock there is a queue peek and one string comparison, the throw itself (arithmetic on a seed), one map "
 "get for the position, the move rule (three numbers in, one out), one map get for the jump, one map put, one deque "
 "rotation and one list append: about ten operations, roughly half a microsecond, and not one of them can block. "
 "Everything slow is outside. The commentary and the websocket push to four phones &mdash; tens of milliseconds "
 "&mdash; happen after the unlock, inside a try/catch, so a screen that has crashed cannot stop the game. When four "
 "phones press roll in the same millisecond, only one of them is going to do any work: the other three discover they "
 "are not on turn after two comparisons, in about a tenth of a microsecond, and go away. So the answer to \"is "
 "everything now one by one?\" is: per table, for half a microsecond, and no human being can tell.", 7),
("Move 8: say the arithmetic, then name the ladder.",
 "Four people at a table throw a die every three seconds or so: a third of a roll per second against a lock held for "
 "half a microsecond, which is a lock busy two hundred-thousandths of one per cent of the time. Scale it the other "
 "way and it is still fine, because the lock is per game: a million live tables doing three hundred thousand rolls a "
 "second between them is a sixth of one core's worth of locked work, spread over a million locks that never meet. Then "
 "the ladder, cheapest first. Screens and persistence stay outside the lock, which this code already does. If you do "
 "not want a thread parked on a lock at all, swap the gate for a turn token: whose turn it is lives in an "
 "<code>AtomicReference</code> and a player moves by winning a compare-and-set, so a refused roll never blocks. Beyond "
 "one process the game becomes a row and the turn becomes a conditional update on the turn number &mdash; the database "
 "doing the same compare-and-set. Say the arithmetic first: climbing this ladder without it is complexity nobody asked "
 "for.", 8),
("Move 9: list what can go wrong, and write the test for each before the interview is over.",
 "Every row in that table is a claim the design makes, and a claim with no test is an opinion. You will not have time "
 "for all eight in an hour, so write them in this order. The race first, because it is the only one that can be wrong "
 "in a way reading cannot catch: eight threads on a latch, then assert the log has one row per accepted roll, the turn "
 "numbers run 1..n with no gap, and every token starts each turn where its own previous turn left it. Then the one "
 "that proves the <i>order</i> inside the turn: hand in a move rule that returns a cell off the board and assert the "
 "position, the log and the turn order are untouched. Then the two rules everybody argues about, the overshoot and the "
 "single jump. The rest &mdash; the bad layout, the broken screen, the undo &mdash; are three lines each once those "
 "three exist. Say the test out loud while you type it: \"this asserts a refused roll does not even spend a die.\"", 9),
("Move 10: now, and only now, name the patterns. Each one is the result of a move.",
 "Read the table as a defence, one sentence each: Strategy because move 3 found three rules that change; Decorator "
 "because two of those rules are better wrapped than copied; Observer because move 4 said a screen must never be "
 "inside the lock; State because move 6 gave the game a life, which is why a roll after the win is refused rather "
 "than quietly appended. Command is the one candidates miss: the <code>Turn</code> record keeps <code>from</code> as "
 "well as <code>to</code>, so the log is an audit trail, a replay script and the undo stack at once, and "
 "<code>undoLastTurn()</code> is fifteen lines with no new state. Builder is the one worth arguing: on most LLDs it is "
 "ceremony and you should say so, but here there are seven optional knobs and four real invariants, "
 "<code>build()</code> is the single place they are checked, and Game's constructor is package-private, so an invalid "
 "game cannot be constructed at all. Factory has not earned its name yet and Singleton never does. A pattern without a "
 "move behind it is decoration.", 10),
("Move 11: run SOLID as a check on the moves, one line each.",
 "SOLID is not a speech; it is the check that the moves did their job, and every letter here points at a line you "
 "already wrote. The one to say out loud is L, because on this problem it is what makes the tests exist: the game "
 "calls <code>dice.roll()</code> and never asks which dice it got, so a test can hand it a script of 6, 6, 2 and "
 "assert an exact board. If any letter has no line behind it, the honest answer is \"not here\" &mdash; a letter you "
 "cannot point at is a letter you did not earn.", 11),
("Move 12: every twist the interviewer adds is one of five moves. Say which before you type.",
 "A new rule (bounce back, reach-or-pass, three dice, a six to get on the board, a bot that picks its dice) is a new "
 "class behind an interface that already exists, plus one line in the builder. Someone new who wants to know (a "
 "scoreboard, analytics, a tournament table) is one more observer. A new step in a life is a checked transition "
 "written under the same lock &mdash; that covers undo, a player who quits and \"miss a turn\", and the last of those "
 "is the one special cell that is <i>not</i> free, because it changes the rotation rather than the position. A new "
 "invariant across players (no two tokens on one cell) is the check and the writes inside the <i>same</i> lock. State "
 "that must outlive the process is a repository behind the positions and the log. For all five the turn loop, the "
 "board and the existing tests do not change, and that is the test that the derivation was right.", 12),
]
DERIVATION_LEAD = ("Run these on any LLD (parking lot, elevator, Splitwise) and the class diagram, the lock, the tests, the "
 "patterns, SOLID and the answer to every twist fall out in that order; nothing is chosen up front, and nothing is named "
 "before the move that produced it. On this problem two moves do most of the work: move 1, where noticing that a snake "
 "and a ladder are one object deletes a whole hierarchy before it is born, and move 6, where the order inside a turn is "
 "the entire difference between a toy and something you would put four phones in front of.")

