HOW_TO_READ = ('<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (dashed border = interface; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed '
 'list of values). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. <b>The arrows.</b> Hollow triangle = '
 'implements. Filled diamond = owns: the game owns the board and every turn, and the board owns its jumps. Plain arrow '
 '= references: a turn points at the jump that carried it. Dashed green = handed in by the builder. Dotted blue = '
 'notifies &mdash; and the builder is the box that hands the game its rules and its clock. <b>Where state lives:</b> everything that moves is on the <code>Game</code> &mdash; every token\'s '
 'position, the turn queue, the log, the counters, the status &mdash; and all of it is behind one '
 '<code>ReentrantLock</code>, which is the whole concurrency answer in one sentence. The board is fixed once '
 '<code>build()</code> has validated it. A player is an id and a name; a jump is two cells; a turn is what happened. '
 'Notice what is <i>not</i> here: no Snake and no Ladder class, because they are one shape with a sign; no Cell class, '
 'because a cell with nothing on it is just a number; and no position field on Player, because the moment two phones '
 'can roll, shared state belongs to whoever holds the lock.')

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ('The green comment above each class and method says what it does; read only those first for the shape, then '
 'the bodies for the mechanics &mdash; and read <code>Game.roll</code> twice, because the order of its lines is the '
 'design. Each copy button copies that whole file for your IDE. Below Main.java: Extensions.java (every follow-up\'s '
 'reference code, with an <code>ExtDemo</code> main that runs all of it) and FailureTests.java (62 checks in ten blocks; '
 '<code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> prints ALL PASS).')

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT_CARD = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3>'
 '<button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div><div class="cb"><div class="prompt">' + PROMPT + '</div>'
 'Before typing, write your six to eight clarifying questions (exact landing and whether jumps chain come first); then '
 'type in the order of Main.java: the two enums, Jump and Player, the Clock, the Dice interface with a standard and a '
 'scripted one, the MoveStrategy interface with its two rules, the ExtraTurnRule with its wrapper, the Turn record, '
 'Board with its validation, then Game with its lock and the order inside <code>roll()</code>, then GameBuilder, then '
 'the thin GameServer, then a main that plays a seeded game and runs the race. If you finish early, add '
 '<code>undoLastTurn()</code> &mdash; it is the fastest way to find out whether your turn loop really wrote things in '
 'one order.</div></div>')

FU = [
("Every roll is a random number. How do you test that, replay a game a player complains about, and let a player be a bot?", "non-functional", 8,
 "The randomness never comes from inside the game. <code>Dice</code> is an interface and the <code>Random</code> is "
 "handed in, so production passes a seeded one and a test passes <code>ScriptedDice</code>, which replays a fixed "
 "sequence and throws if the test asks for one roll more than it scripted. That is what makes an assertion like "
 "\"after a 4 the token is on the ladder foot at 4 and therefore rests on 20\" possible at all, and it is how the race "
 "test proves a refused request does not even spend a die: the dice counts what it handed out. A bug report is then "
 "two lines, the seed and the layout &mdash; the last failure test plays a seeded game twice and asserts the same "
 "rolls, the same board and the same winner. The bot rides the same seam: <code>BotDice</code> looks six cells ahead "
 "through the board it was handed and throws one die when a ladder is in reach, two when the stretch is full of "
 "snakes, and the game cannot tell, because all it ever calls is <code>dice.roll()</code>. One smell worth naming "
 "rather than hiding: the bot reads its own position back through a supplier, and if it grows, pass the position into "
 "<code>roll()</code> instead.",
 sect(src, "final class ScriptedDice implements Dice {", "interface MoveStrategy {") + "\n" + X("a bot that chooses", "special cells")),

("Two phones press roll at the same instant. Prove a token cannot move twice, with a test.", "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between \"it is Bob's turn\" and \"Bob has moved\". <code>roll()</code> does the whole thing &mdash; "
 "the state check, the turn check, the throw, the move and the rotation &mdash; inside one lock held by the game, so "
 "no second caller can get into that gap. The proof is two tests. Eight threads wait on a latch and all roll for the "
 "same, correct player: exactly one is accepted, the token moved exactly once, the turn passed exactly once. Then four "
 "hundred attempts from eight threads roll for random players, and the log has to hold up: one row per accepted roll, "
 "turn numbers 1..n with no gap, every token starting each turn where its own previous turn left it, and replaying the "
 "log reproducing the live board.",
 T("        // 1. four phones press roll over and over at the same instant.", "        // 3. a ladder lifts")),

("One lock per game. Does that scale, or have you serialised the server?", "non-functional", 5,
 "It scales, and the answer is arithmetic, not an opinion. The locked part is about ten operations &mdash; a queue "
 "peek, a string compare, the throw, two map gets, a map put, a rotation and a list append &mdash; roughly half a "
 "microsecond, with nothing in it that can block. Four people at a table roll about a third of a time a second, so "
 "that lock is busy about two hundred-thousandths of one per cent. And it is per game: a million tables doing three "
 "hundred thousand rolls a second between them is about a sixth of a core, spread over a million separate locks that "
 "never meet. If you want no thread ever parked, <code>TurnToken</code> keeps whose turn it is in an "
 "<code>AtomicReference</code> and a player moves by winning a compare-and-set, so a roll out of turn is refused "
 "without blocking.",
 X("the lock-free turn gate", "online play")),

("A six rolls again — but not more than three times in a row. Add it without touching Game.", "twist", 8,
 "The extra-turn rule is already an interface, and it is pure: the game hands it the roll and how many extras this "
 "player has had in a row, and it answers yes or no. So \"a six rolls again\" is <code>ExtraTurnOnMax</code>, and the "
 "cap is not a copy of it &mdash; it is a wrapper, <code>CappedExtraTurn</code>, which asks the inner rule and then "
 "refuses once the count reaches the cap. That is the Decorator, and it works on any extra-turn rule ever written. The "
 "game's part is two lines: when the rule says yes it does not rotate the queue and increments the counter, and "
 "anything else resets the counter to zero. The test throws three sixes and asserts the turn passes on the third.",
 sect(src, "interface ExtraTurnRule {", "record Turn(")),

("Must you land exactly on the last cell? And what if you need a six to get on the board at all?", "functional", 5,
 "Both are the same seam, which is why neither costs a line inside <code>Game</code>. <code>OvershootStays</code> is "
 "the classic rule: a throw that would pass the last cell is wasted and the token stays. <code>BounceBack</code> sends "
 "the surplus steps back off the end, and <code>ReachOrPass</code> clamps the landing in one line. The entry rule is "
 "not a fourth copy: <code>MustRollSixToEnter</code> <i>wraps</i> whichever rule you already had and answers 0 while "
 "the token is still off the board, so \"a six to start\" and \"bounce back off 100\" compose for free. The win check "
 "never changes either &mdash; <code>to == board.size()</code> on the cell the token came to rest on &mdash; so a "
 "ladder onto the last cell wins too. And if they want second and third place rather than stopping at the first "
 "winner, the winner simply leaves the queue, which is what <code>leave</code> already does.",
 sect(src, "interface MoveStrategy {", "// \"and if you roll a six") + "\n" + X("the two house rules", "a bot that chooses")),

("A rule you were handed gives back a cell that is not on the board. What is the state of the game?", "functional", 8,
 "Exactly what it was. Nothing is written until the whole turn has been worked out: the throw, the landing cell, the "
 "jump and the win are computed into local variables, and both the landing and the destination are checked against the "
 "board before the first <code>put</code>. So a broken move rule throws, the token has not moved, the log has no row, "
 "the queue has not rotated and the same player is still due &mdash; the client simply rolls again. The test hands in "
 "a rule that returns <code>size + 99</code> and asserts all four. The one thing that is spent is the die itself, "
 "which is honest: the throw already happened, exactly as it does when somebody knocks the board over after rolling.",
 M("Turn roll(String playerId) {", "Turn forfeitTurn(String playerId)")),

("The screen asks \"what is on cell 47?\" and \"whose turn is it?\" a thousand times a second.", "non-functional", 5,
 "Both are already one hop, and no new structure is needed. A landing resolves through "
 "<code>Map&lt;Integer, Jump&gt;</code>, which stores only the twenty-odd cells that do something rather than a "
 "hundred objects of which eighty are empty. Whose turn it is, is <code>peek</code> on an <code>ArrayDeque</code>; "
 "rotating is <code>poll</code> then <code>addLast</code>, both O(1), which is why the turn order is a queue and not a "
 "list with an index somebody has to maintain. A position is a map get. These reads take the game's lock so a caller "
 "never sees a half-applied turn, which costs tens of nanoseconds; and if a UI later needs a picture per cell, a dense "
 "array of cells becomes the better home and nothing else changes.",
 sect(src, "class Board {", "class OutOfTurnException")),

("\"Take that move back\" — the phone fired twice and the wrong roll went in.", "twist", 8,
 "Undo is cheap only because of the order in move 6: every write of a turn goes through one method, in one order, so "
 "undo is that list read upwards. Under the same lock it puts the position back to the turn's <code>from</code>, "
 "brings the jump counter down, drops the log row, unmakes the win if there was one, and rotates the queue backwards "
 "if that turn rotated it. The extra-turn counter is not stored anywhere else either &mdash; it is read back off the "
 "log, by counting the trailing turns of that player that granted an extra. Two things it deliberately does not do: "
 "it does not un-throw the die, because the throw really happened and pretending otherwise would break a seeded "
 "replay, and it does not tell the observers, because <code>onTurn</code> means a turn happened. Telling them would "
 "mean a second method on <code>GameObserver</code>, and a two-method interface is a real cost &mdash; name it rather "
 "than pretend it is free.",
 M("Turn undoLastTurn() {", "void leave(String playerId)")),

("Special cells: land here and teleport, land there and jump forward, land on that one and miss a turn.", "twist", 8,
 "Two of the three are free and one is not, and saying which is the answer. The game asks the board exactly one "
 "question &mdash; what happens to a token that rests on this cell &mdash; so a teleport cell and a boost cell are a "
 "<code>Board</code> subclass that answers with a jump it made up, and the turn loop never learns they exist. Its "
 "randomness comes from the same injected <code>Random</code> as the dice, or replay stops working. Miss a turn is "
 "different in kind, because it changes the rotation rather than the position: it needs one field on the game, the set "
 "of players to skip, and one line at the top of <code>roll()</code>. That is move 12's third twist, a new step in a "
 "life, and it costs about three lines.",
 X("special cells", "turn timers")),

("Make it a real online game: many tables, a request that gets retried, and a phone that disconnects.", "twist", 10,
 "Three separate things. Many tables: <code>GameServer</code> is a map from game id to game and holds no lock of its "
 "own, so the shard key is the game id and two tables never contend; an out-of-turn roll comes back as its own "
 "exception type, so the server answers 409 rather than 500. A retried request: the request id is the key &mdash; "
 "<code>IdempotentRolls</code> plays it once and hands the same <code>Turn</code> back to every later copy, and "
 "<code>computeIfAbsent</code> is what makes that hold under a race. A phone that disappears for good: "
 "<code>leave</code> takes the same lock, pulls the player out of the turn queue so the turn passes on at once if it "
 "was theirs, keeps their log rows because history has to stay true, and ends the game when one player is left "
 "standing. The honest caveat on the retry is that a crash between the roll and the remembering loses the record; only "
 "the database version below fixes that.",
 X("online play", "persistence") + "\n" + M("void leave(String playerId)", "Player playOut(")),

("Persist it. And now there are two servers.", "twist", 5,
 "The positions and the log go behind a repository interface and the game's code does not change, only what it was "
 "handed. The turn number becomes the version: a turn is written only if the stored game is still on the turn the "
 "caller thinks it is on, which is <code>UPDATE game SET turn_no = turn_no + 1 ... WHERE game_id = ? AND turn_no = ?</code>. "
 "Zero rows updated means somebody else moved first, so re-read and try again &mdash; the database doing exactly what "
 "the <code>ReentrantLock</code> did in one process, across as many servers as you like. The turn row carries the "
 "request id with a unique index on it, so an at-least-once delivery applies once.",
 X("persistence", "Runs every extension")),

("Where does time come from, and how does a player who has not rolled in thirty seconds lose the turn?", "design", 3,
 "The game has a <code>Clock</code> it was handed and stamps every turn with it; nothing else reads the wall clock. A "
 "test hands in a clock that returns a fixed instant and moves it by hand, so thirty seconds pass in no time at all. "
 "The timer itself is not in the game: it is a small object wired in as an observer, so every turn tells it who is on "
 "the clock now, and either a sweeper thread or the next request asks whether that player is late. When they are, it "
 "calls <code>forfeitTurn</code>, which takes the same lock, writes a turn row with a roll of 0 and rotates the queue "
 "&mdash; a forfeit is a turn, so it is in the log and in the replay like everything else.",
 X("turn timers", "the lock-free turn gate")),

("Which pattern is where, which SOLID letter is where, and where would a Factory earn its place?", "design", 8,
 "None of them was chosen up front; each is what a move produced, and that is how to say it. Strategy is move 3: the "
 "dice, the move rule and the extra-turn rule are the three things that will change. Decorator is the same move twice "
 "over &mdash; <code>CappedExtraTurn</code> wraps an extra-turn rule, <code>MustRollSixToEnter</code> wraps a move "
 "rule. Observer is move 4's rule that a screen is never inside the lock; State is move 6, which is why a roll after "
 "the win is refused; Command is move 6 again, because the <code>Turn</code> record keeps <code>from</code> as well as "
 "<code>to</code>, which is what makes <code>undoLastTurn()</code> possible with no extra state. Builder is the one to "
 "argue: on most LLDs it is ceremony, but here there are seven optional knobs and four invariants, "
 "<code>build()</code> is the one place they are checked, and Game's constructor is package-private so an invalid game "
 "cannot exist. Factory has not earned its name &mdash; <code>dice(count, faces)</code> is already the registry, and "
 "it earns the name the day rules arrive as configuration strings.",
 "// S: one reason to change each\n"
 "Jump   jumpAt(int cell)                       // Board: what is on a cell\n"
 "int    roll()                                 // Dice: a number\n"
 "Turn   roll(String playerId)                  // Game: the turn loop, and the lock\n\n"
 "// O + L: a new rule is a new file; the caller never asks which one it got\n"
 "final class ReachOrPass implements MoveStrategy {\n"
 "    public int landingCell(int from, int roll, int size) { return Math.min(from + roll, size); }\n"
 "}\n"
 "new GameBuilder().moveRule(new ReachOrPass())         // the only line that changes\n\n"
 "// Decorator: a rule wrapping a rule, on both interfaces\n"
 "new CappedExtraTurn(new ExtraTurnOnMax(6), 3)\n"
 "new MustRollSixToEnter(new OvershootStays())\n\n"
 "// I: one method each, so a test double is a lambda\n"
 "interface Dice          { int roll(); }\n"
 "interface MoveStrategy  { int landingCell(int from, int roll, int boardSize); }\n"
 "interface ExtraTurnRule { boolean rollAgain(int roll, int extrasSoFar); }\n"
 "interface GameObserver  { void onTurn(String gameId, Turn t); }\n"
 "interface Clock         { long nowMs(); }\n\n"
 "// D: everything is handed in, which is what makes the failure tests possible\n"
 "new GameBuilder().dice(new ScriptedDice(6, 6, 2)).clock(() -> now[0])\n"
 "                 .moveRule((from, roll, size) -> size + 99)   // deliberately broken, in a test\n"),
]

build(dict(
